      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18} & =2 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =-5,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $(-5,0)$ and $(7,0).$",
